    "scripture": "Acts 28:17–22; Acts 24:14–16; Acts 26:6–8; Romans 9:1–5; Romans 10:1–4",
    "narrative": [
     "After settling in Rome, Paul did not remain silent while waiting for his case to proceed.",
     "Three days after his arrival, he called together the leading Jewish men in the city.",
     "This meeting was significant. Paul was himself a Jewish man who had been trained in the Scriptures. His understanding of Jesus Christ did not lead him to treat his people with contempt. Instead, he sought an opportunity to explain his position and discuss the hope connected with Israel’s Scriptures.",
     "Paul began by addressing the circumstances that had brought him to Rome.",
     "He explained that he had done nothing against his people or the customs inherited from his ancestors. Nevertheless, he had been delivered as a prisoner from Jerusalem into Roman hands.",
     "He told them that the Roman authorities had examined him and wanted to release him because they had found no basis for putting him to death.",
     "However, opposition from some of the Jewish leaders had complicated the situation. Paul had therefore appealed to Caesar—not because he wanted to bring an accusation against his own nation, but because he needed his case to be heard.",
     "Paul also explained the reason he wanted to speak with them: he was bound because of the hope of Israel.",
     "That hope centred on God’s promises and their fulfilment in Jesus Christ.",
     "The Jewish leaders responded that they had not received letters from Judea about Paul, nor had anyone arrived with a formal report accusing him. They had also not received a direct report from anyone who had come to Rome.",
     "However, they knew that the Christian movement was spoken against in various places.",
     "They therefore wanted to hear Paul’s own explanation of his beliefs.",
     "This conversation reveals an important part of Paul’s character. Even after enduring serious opposition, he was willing to engage in a respectful discussion with people who wanted to understand his position.",
     "He did not begin by attacking everyone who disagreed with him. He explained his circumstances, clarified misunderstandings, and opened the way for a deeper conversation about Scripture.",
     "Paul’s example is especially relevant when Christians face misunderstanding.",
     "A faithful witness must be prepared to explain what they believe and why they believe it. That does not mean every conversation will end in agreement. It means we should seek truth, speak honestly, and represent Christ faithfully.",
     "Paul had endured suffering because of his message, but he still regarded the opportunity to explain that message as valuable.",
     "He wanted his listeners to understand that the hope he proclaimed was not a rejection of God’s promises to Israel. Rather, he believed that God’s promises found their fulfilment in Jesus."
    ],
