     "In John 7, Jesus was in Galilee while opposition against Him was increasing in Judea. His brothers urged Him to go to Judea so that His disciples could see the works He was doing. Their words suggested that they did not fully understand His mission or the divine purpose behind His ministry.",
     "The situation reveals an important truth: physical proximity to spiritual things does not automatically produce spiritual understanding.",
     "James had access to a unique family relationship with Jesus, but that relationship did not remove the need for personal faith.",
     "A person may grow up attending church, live in a Christian family, know biblical stories, or spend time around respected Christian leaders and still need to come to a personal understanding of Jesus Christ.",
     "Faith cannot be inherited in the same way that a family name is inherited. Each person must respond to the truth of Christ.",
     "The Gospels do not provide a detailed account of every conversation between James and Jesus during those years. We should therefore avoid inventing private family scenes or claiming to know precisely what James thought at every stage.",
     "What Scripture does tell us is significant enough: James did not initially believe in Jesus, yet his later life became closely associated with the Christian movement.",
     "His story reminds us that a person’s present response to God does not necessarily determine the rest of their spiritual journey.",
     "Someone who doubts today may believe tomorrow. Someone who appears uninterested may eventually become a faithful servant of Christ.",
     "We must therefore resist the temptation to write people off because of their current attitudes.",
