     "Its opening words reach back to the beginning, declaring that the Word existed in the beginning, was with God, and was God.",
     "The Gospel then describes the Word becoming flesh and dwelling among humanity.",
     "This opening establishes the central concern of John’s testimony: Jesus is not merely a teacher who offered moral guidance. He is the Word who became flesh, the One who reveals the Father and brings life to those who believe.",
     "Throughout the Gospel, Jesus speaks about His identity and mission.",
     "He describes Himself as the bread of life, the light of the world, the door, the good shepherd, the resurrection and the life, the way, the truth, and the life, and the true vine.",
     "These descriptions are not disconnected titles. Each reveals something about who Jesus is and what He accomplishes.",
     "The Gospel also records signs that point toward His identity, including the healing of the sick, the feeding of the multitude, the giving of sight to a man born blind, and the raising of Lazarus.",
     "Yet John does not present the signs as spectacles meant merely to impress people.",
     "They serve the larger purpose of revealing the glory of Jesus and calling people to believe in Him.",
     "Near the end of the Gospel, Thomas tells the other disciples that he will not believe that Jesus has risen unless he sees the marks of the nails and places his hand in Jesus’s side.",
