  "title": "Thomas the Apostle: From Doubt to Faith",
  "subtitle": null,
  "body": [
   "Topic Description",
   "Thomas, one of the twelve apostles of Jesus Christ, is often remembered as the disciple who doubted the resurrection. Yet his story reveals much more than a moment of uncertainty. It reveals the journey of a sincere disciple who struggled to understand, desired truth, encountered the risen Christ, and confessed Jesus as his Lord and God.",
   "Through Thomas, we learn that questions can become opportunities for deeper understanding, that faith must be grounded in the truth of Christ, and that encountering Jesus can transform uncertainty into conviction.",
   "Thomas’s life also challenges believers to remain faithful during difficult circumstances, to seek understanding rather than pretend, and to respond to the truth of the resurrection with wholehearted commitment.",
   "CENTRAL THEME: Moving from uncertainty to a personal, confident faith in Jesus Christ.",
   "PRIMARY SCRIPTURES: John 11:1–16; John 14:1–7; John 20:19–29; John 21:1–14.",
   "SUPPORTING SCRIPTURES: Matthew 28:1–10; Mark 16:9–14; Luke 24:36–49; Acts 1:12–14; 1 Peter 1:6–9.",
   "Learning Objectives",
   "By the end of this topic, learners should be able to:",
   "1. Explain Thomas’s role among the disciples of Jesus.",
   "2. Understand Thomas’s response to the death and resurrection of Christ.",
   "3. Identify the difference between honest questions and a persistent refusal to believe.",
   "4. Explain the significance of Thomas’s confession, “My Lord and my God.”",
   "5. Apply biblical principles of faith, courage, humility, and perseverance.",
   "6. Encourage others who are struggling with questions about their faith.",
   "7. Demonstrate how personal conviction in Christ should influence everyday life.",
   "Comprehensive Check — Thomas the Apostle: From Doubt to Faith",
   "Answer the following questions in your own words, using Scripture to support your responses.",
   "1. Who was Thomas, and what was his role among the disciples of Jesus?",
   "2. What did Thomas say when Jesus decided to return to Judea after Lazarus became sick?",
   "3. What does Thomas’s response in John 11:16 reveal about his willingness to accompany Jesus?",
   "4. What question did Thomas ask in John 14:5?",
   "5. How did Jesus respond to Thomas’s question about the way?",
   "6. Why did the death of Jesus create such a crisis for the disciples?",
   "7. What did Mary Magdalene report to the disciples after visiting the tomb?",
   "8. Why was Thomas absent when Jesus first appeared to the disciples in John 20?",
   "9. What conditions did Thomas set before he would believe that Jesus had risen?",
   "10. How did Jesus respond when He appeared to the disciples again?",
   "11. What did Thomas confess after encountering the risen Christ?",
   "12. What does the confession “My Lord and my God” reveal about Thomas’s recognition of Jesus?",
   "13. What did Jesus teach about those who believe without having seen Him?",
   "14. Why is the resurrection central to the Christian faith?",
   "15. What does Thomas’s presence among the apostles in Acts 1 teach us about his continued place in the apostolic community?",
   "16. What is the difference between honest questions and a persistent refusal to accept truth?",
   "17. How can Christians support people who are struggling with questions about faith?",
   "18. What practical lessons can believers learn from Thomas’s willingness to follow Jesus despite danger?",
   "19. How should a personal confession of Jesus as Lord influence a believer’s everyday life?",
   "20. What is the central lesson of Thomas’s journey from uncertainty to faith?",
   "Final Assignment — My Response to the Risen Christ",
   "Prepare a written, audio, or video reflection titled:",
   "“My Lord and My God: Responding to Jesus Christ.”",
   "Your work should address the following areas.",
   "Part 1 — Understanding Thomas",
   "Explain Thomas’s journey through the Gospel accounts. Include his response to the danger surrounding the journey to Judea, his question in John 14, his struggle to accept the resurrection report, and his encounter with Jesus.",
   "Part 2 — Understanding Jesus",
   "Explain why the resurrection matters to Christian faith. Discuss the significance of Jesus’s words in John 14:6 and Thomas’s confession in John 20:28.",
   "Part 3 — Personal Reflection",
   "Reflect honestly on the questions you have faced about faith. You do not need to disclose private or painful experiences to other people. Focus on what you have learned and how you intend to continue seeking truth through Scripture, prayer, and Christian fellowship.",
   "Part 4 — Practical Application",
   "Identify three practical actions you will take to strengthen your faith and help others grow in their understanding of Jesus.",
   "Part 5 — Peer Discipleship",
   "Prepare a short encouragement for someone who is struggling with doubt. Use Scripture, explain the hope found in Jesus Christ, and demonstrate patience and compassion.",
   "Submission Options",
   "Choose the method that works best for you:",
   "• Type your response.",
   "• Write your response by hand and upload a clear photograph or scan.",
   "• Use Apple Pencil or another compatible stylus on a supported device.",
   "• Upload a supported document.",
   "• Record an audio or video reflection, if enabled for the assignment.",
   "• Save a private reflection without submitting it for review, where the learning workspace supports this option.",
   "Your submission should demonstrate understanding, personal reflection, and practical application. You do not need to disclose sensitive personal information to demonstrate what you have learned.",
   "Memory Verse",
   "John 20:28 (KJV)",
   "“And Thomas answered and said unto him, My Lord and my God.”",
   "Memory Verse Reflection",
   "Thomas’s confession reveals a personal recognition of Jesus Christ. He did not merely acknowledge that Jesus had risen; he addressed Him as his Lord and God.",
   "This confession challenges every believer to consider whether Jesus is simply someone they know about or the Lord whose truth governs their life.",
   "To call Jesus my Lord means recognizing His authority and responding to His teaching with obedience. To call Him my God means acknowledging His divine identity and giving Him the honour He deserves.",
   "Personal reflection: What would change in your decisions, relationships, priorities, and conduct if you consistently lived in the light of your confession that Jesus is your Lord and God?",
   "Closing Prayer",
   "Heavenly Father,",
   "Thank You for revealing Yourself through Jesus Christ and for giving us the testimony of His life, death, and resurrection.",
   "Thank You for the story of Thomas, which reminds us that our questions do not have to be the end of our journey. Teach us to seek truth sincerely, to study Your Word carefully, and to remain humble when we do not understand.",
   "Lord Jesus, help us to recognize You as the way, the truth, and the life. Strengthen our faith in times of uncertainty. When we face questions, guide us toward understanding. When we experience fear, give us courage. When we are discouraged, remind us of the hope secured through Your resurrection.",
   "Forgive us when we allow fear, pride, or personal expectations to prevent us from trusting Your Word. Give us hearts that are willing to receive Your truth and lives that demonstrate our commitment to You.",
   "Help us to encourage those who are struggling with faith. Teach us to listen patiently, speak truthfully, and respond with compassion. May our words and actions point people toward Jesus rather than toward ourselves.",
   "May our confession be sincere: My Lord and my God.",
   "Let our faith produce obedience, our knowledge produce humility, and our discipleship produce faithful service to others.",
   "In Jesus Christ’s name, Amen.TOPIC 23 — THOMAS THE APOSTLE: FROM DOUBT TO FAITH"
  ],
